WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.528
COVID-19 is spreading rapidly

00:00:02.528 --> 00:00:03.860
throughout the world.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.910
The US has reported
over 5,000 cases so far,

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:10.620
with many states practicing
social distancing

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.770
to limit the spread of the virus.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.997
The Centers for Disease
Control and Prevention

00:00:14.997 --> 00:00:17.430
say that COVID-19 is spread primarily

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.740
through person-to-person contact,

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.770
from something like a cough or a sneeze.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.020
But even if you're not in direct contact

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.680
with someone who is contagious,

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.460
you might still come in
contact with the virus.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.120
If someone coughs or
sneezes into their hands

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.950
or wipes their nose and
then touches a surface,

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.730
it could become contaminated.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.937
The CDC says this type of transmission

00:00:38.937 --> 00:00:40.810
"has not been documented."

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.068
But experts say contaminated surfaces

00:00:43.068 --> 00:00:45.170
can still pose a risk.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.680
New research from the
National Institutes of Health,

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:48.880
Princeton University,

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.310
and the University of
California, Los Angeles,

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.830
broke down how long the COVID-19 virus

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:56.750
can survive on different surfaces.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.425
And while these results could be affected

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:01.460
by temperature, humidity,
UV light, and wind,

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.475
it's a reminder of how
easily the virus can spread.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.390
Luckily, the virus isn't airborne.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:13.330
But it can linger in droplets
on particles in the air.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.410
The study found that viruses could last

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.110
up to three hours in droplets in the air.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.710
Maintaining a distance of
six feet from other people

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:24.003
can help avoid particles spread
through a cough or a sneeze.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.560
On copper surfaces, like a drawer handle,

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.986
the virus can last up to four hours.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.182
While researchers found that the virus

00:01:37.182 --> 00:01:40.120
can survive on cardboard
for up to 24 hours,

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.420
the CDC believes packages or mail

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.638
are unlikely to transmit the virus.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.597
COVID-19 appears to last the longest

00:01:50.597 --> 00:01:54.740
on plastic and stainless
steel, two to three days.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.900
That's longer than the
flu can live on surfaces,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.150
according to the CDC.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.620
Nonporous surfaces seem to allow the virus

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.570
to survive longer.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.660
That could include things like doorknobs,

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.748
bus and subway handrails,

00:02:07.748 --> 00:02:10.850
light switches, desks, and keyboards.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.620
We don't know how long the virus can live

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.840
on a phone or a phone case,

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.967
but researchers speculate
that because it's

00:02:16.967 --> 00:02:19.100
a smooth, nonabsorbent surface,

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.720
it's possible that the survivability

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.498
would be similar to plastic.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:25.750
There are several steps you can take

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.960
to reduce your risk of exposure.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.940
Always avoid touching your
eyes, mouth, and face,

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.740
and wash your hands with soap and water

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:35.280
for at least 20 seconds.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.046
If you're not able to wash your hands,

00:02:37.046 --> 00:02:38.650
use hand sanitizer.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.341
You should also routinely clean

00:02:40.341 --> 00:02:41.940
commonly used surfaces,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.806
like doorknobs and your phone,

00:02:43.806 --> 00:02:45.990
with disinfecting wipes.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.930
And avoid nonessential
travel to public places,

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.013
especially at peak times.

